arm64: dts: mediatek: gemini: add the Novatek touchscreen node

The node the whole touchscreen depends on was never in this series. It was
added by hand to the build tree when the driver landed and tracked nowhere,
so applying the patch series produced a kernel with the driver compiled in
and nothing for it to bind to. Found 2026-08-21 by applying the series to a
pristine tree and diffing against the tree that actually builds.

The vendor DTB carries BOTH touch controllers on this bus — cap_touch@62
(Novatek) and solomon_touch@53 — because the Gemini shipped in two panel
variants with a different touch IC each, and the vendor kernel builds a
driver for both and lets probe decide. Keep both enabled for the same
reason: one image, either unit. On this device 0x53 answers with -ENXIO and
0x62 answers; on bsg100's it is the other way round.

Two things in here are not obvious and are commented in place: the chip is
addressed at 0x62 but its reports are read from 0x01, and CTP_INT is an
ordinary GPIO rather than an interrupt because mainline's MT6797 pinctrl
registers no EINT data at all (B-11).

`panel = <&gemini_panel>` makes the driver a drm_panel_follower: this is a
TDDI part, so the panel's prepare/unprepare IS this controller's power
sequence.

Signed-off-by: mratan <mattdratan@gmail.com>

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt6797-gemini-pda.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt6797-gemini-pda.dts
@@ -577,6 +577,49 @@
 	clock-frequency = <400000>;
 	status = "okay";
 
+	/*
+	 * VARIANT (2026-08-20): this unit is the Novatek one. The vendor DTB
+	 * carries BOTH touch controllers on this bus — cap_touch@62 (Novatek)
+	 * and solomon_touch@53 — because the Gemini shipped with two panel
+	 * variants, each with its own touch IC, and the vendor kernel builds a
+	 * driver for each and lets probe decide.
+	 *
+	 * bsg100's device is the Solomon one: their scan found 0x53 ACKing and
+	 * 0x62 absent. On this device it is the other way round — the panel is
+	 * an NT36672 and `ssd2092 3-0053` fails with -ENXIO, i.e. nothing is at
+	 * 0x53. Leaving both nodes enabled means one image serves both units,
+	 * which is what the vendor does.
+	 *
+	 * The chip is addressed at 0x62 but reports are read from 0x01; the
+	 * driver handles that. See gemini-nt36xxx.c.
+	 */
+	cap_touch: touchscreen@62 {
+		compatible = "novatek,nt36xxx-ts";
+		reg = <0x62>;	/* vendor DTB cap_touch@62 */
+		reset-gpios = <&pio 68 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;	/* CTP_RST, shared with the Solomon node */
+		/*
+		 * CTP_INT as a plain GPIO, not an interrupt: mainline MT6797
+		 * pinctrl registers no EINT data (B-11), so `interrupts` here
+		 * resolves to nothing. The driver reads this line to know when
+		 * the point buffer is valid — without that gate the reads
+		 * return junk.
+		 */
+		irq-gpios = <&pio 85 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;		/* CTP_INT, data-ready */
+		/* Clamshell used in landscape; the panel is natively portrait. */
+		touchscreen-swapped-x-y;
+		touchscreen-inverted-y;
+		/*
+		 * This is a TDDI part: the touch controller shares the panel's
+		 * die and its power domain, so the panel's prepare/unprepare is
+		 * this chip's power sequence. Naming the panel here makes the
+		 * driver a drm_panel_follower, which is how it learns to stop
+		 * polling an unpowered chip and to re-initialise one that has
+		 * just come back from cold.
+		 */
+		panel = <&gemini_panel>;
+		status = "okay";
+	};
+
 	touchscreen@53 {
 		compatible = "solomon,ssd2092-touch";
 		reg = <0x53>;	/* vendor DTB solomon_touch@53 */
